"Models a broader human activity across contexts and providers to reveal needs beyond one organization's existing offer.\n\nUse when: Explore unmet needs before defining a particular service. Compare common stages across several people's real experiences.\n\nAvoid when: The question is a tightly scoped interaction with a named product.\n\nVisual grammar: Stages represent the broader activity rather than company touchpoints. Lanes capture actions, needs, thoughts and contextual factors. Evidence notes retain meaningful variations instead of forcing a single universal path.\n\nBuild: Define the human goal without assuming the eventual solution. Gather accounts spanning the full activity and relevant contexts. Synthesize shared stages and distinguish significant alternative paths.\n\nCheck: Stages remain independent of one brand's internal process. Variations are grounded in actual research participants. The map states which population and contexts its synthesis covers.\n\nPitfalls: Turning the map into an idealized product funnel. Claiming one sequence applies to everyone without investigating variation.\n\nExample: A learning-to-cycle map spans choosing equipment, finding safe practice, gaining confidence and maintaining the bicycle across multiple providers.\n\nAlternatives: Customer journey map, Mental model diagram."
